  "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{\u2033}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}Y(s).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
